Faithful Reader:

A lurker on ARS pointed out some strange chain of events
as it relates to Interpol:

I am curious about something you posted recently: 
 
>  I think they went directly to Miscavige's ol' 
>chum, Interpol Secretary General R. E. Kendall in Lyon,  
>France.  This guy has distributed scieno PR/propaganda  
>for them worldwide. 
 
If this is true, the following sequence of events is 
truly fascinating: 
 
1990 - the National Commission on Law Enforcement and Social  
Justice, a group apparently set up by Scientology, claims  
that money spent to support Interpol has been expediting the  
flow of drugs into the United States. 
 
1990 - The Church of Scientology publishes a book entitled  
"Interpol -- Private Group, Public Menace", dead-agenting  
the Interpol. 
 
1990 - Freedom magazine publishes an "expose" of Interpol's  
"corrupt activities".  In particular, Freedom "exposes" that  
Interpol's Secretary General Raymond Kendall awarded the ex- 
dictator of Panama, Manuel Noriega, Interpol's most  
distinguished award several months prior to Noriega's  
indictment for drug trafficking and money laundering. 

[1990 (I am adding this)]  A famous hacker rumored to be hired by the 
cult through two Los Angeles private investigators--one with Federal
law enforcement background.  The hacker is successful in wiretapping
a high level defector of Scientology--as well as other high level
wiretaps.  The FBI is rumored to have arrested the hacker with the
wiretap tapes in his possession, and had transcriptions made; yet,
the FBI curiously never prosecutes this--a possible cover-up.  The
FBI suggests that prosecuting for wiretaps in low in the bureau's 
priorities, and doesn't waste manpower for '139's.  They cite that
the IRS has Scientology listed as an "exclusively charitable and 
religious organization that benefits society as a whole."  They 
suggest this to be the truth.  We do not know to what extent the 
hacker was able to penetrate other sensitive communications channels.
 
1990/91 - The National Association of Chiefs of Police  
conducts a non-scientific poll as part of an  
"investigation" apparently prompted by the Church of  
Scientology.  The Police chiefs surveyed were sent copies of  
the Scientology book, "Interpol -- Private Group, Public  
Menace", and were asked to read the book and then answer the  
survey questions.   

1991 (I am adding this):  The FBI mysteriously drops a criminal
RICO investigation they have going on Scientology.  Gene Ingram
tries to serve papers on one of the investigating Special Agents
at his home, in 1989/90.  [See Time Magazine May 6, 1991, Cover
story by Richard Behar].
  
From that point on, the National Association of Chiefs of  
Police and its president, Gerald Arenberg, lead an  
international campaign against Interpol and U.S. support of  
the police organization, citing the findings in the  
Scientology book and its own survey. 
 
1993 - Gerald Arenberg receives a Freedom Human Rights  
Leadership Medal (presented by Heber C. Jentzsch) for his  
"international campaign to expose corruption within  
Interpol, the private and unregulated International Criminal  
Police Organization which Arenberg says is responsible for  
much of the drug trafficking that endangers the lives of  
police officers." 
 
If you are right, the reversal in the relationship between  
Interpol and Scientology is extraordinary.  Are you  
sure you are right about this? 
  
Yes.	
